course: chinese-street
lesson: 32
title: Olympiad: measure words and characters as pictures ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In English you say «a cup of tea» or «a sheet of paper»; in Mandarin almost every counted thing needs a little word like that between the number and the noun, as in yí ge rén, one person, and liǎng ge píngguǒ, two apples.
explain: ge is the everyday all-rounder, but long bendy things take tiáo, flat things take zhāng, books take běn and many animals take zhī, so the measure word paints a little picture of the shape.
explain: Some characters began as drawings: 人 is a person walking, 山 is a mountain with three peaks, 木 is a tree with its branches and roots, and 日 was once a round sun.
explain: Put picture-characters together and you get new ideas: two trees 林 make a wood, a sun and a moon 明 make «bright», and a person leaning on a tree 休 is having a rest, the xiū in xiūxi.
explain: But careful, detectives: most characters are not pictures at all, they join a part for the meaning with a part that hints at the sound, so a picture guess is a good start, not a sure answer.
fact: A measure word goes between the number and the noun: sān běn shū is three books and liǎng zhāng zhǐ is two sheets of paper.
fact: ge is the most common measure word, used for people and many things, as in yí ge rén and liǎng ge píngguǒ (two apples); in fast talk people use ge a lot.
fact: tiáo is for long, thin, bendy things: yì tiáo yú (a fish), yì tiáo lù (a road), yì tiáo hé (a river), yì tiáo kùzi (a pair of trousers), and also snakes (shé) and dragons.
fact: zhāng is for flat things with a big surface: yì zhāng zhǐ (paper), yì zhāng zhàopiàn (a photo), yì zhāng zhuōzi (a table), yì zhāng chuáng (a bed).
fact: běn is for books and things bound like books: yì běn shū (a book), yì běn zìdiǎn (a dictionary).
fact: zhī is for many animals, such as yì zhī māo (a cat), yì zhī niǎo (a bird) and yì zhī tùzi (a rabbit); a dog can take zhī or tiáo.
fact: bēi means a cup or glass of something: yì bēi shuǐ (a glass of water), liǎng bēi nǎichá (two milk teas).
fact: kǒu (mouth) counts the people in a family: nǐ jiā yǒu jǐ kǒu rén? asks how many people are in your family, and wǒ jiā yǒu sì kǒu rén means there are four.
fact: Picture-characters: 人 rén person, 口 kǒu mouth, 山 shān mountain, 木 mù tree, 日 rì sun or day, 月 yuè moon or month, 水 shuǐ water, 火 huǒ fire.
fact: Characters built from pictures: 林 lín (two trees) is a wood, 森 sēn (three trees) is in sēnlín, a forest, 明 míng (sun and moon) means bright, and 休 xiū (person and tree) means rest.
fact: 一 yī, 二 èr and 三 sān are one, two and three lines, and 十 shí (ten) is a cross, like crossed fingers for ten.
fact: 好 hǎo (good) is made of 女 (woman) and 子 (child), and 大 dà (big) is a person with arms stretched out wide.
fact: Only a small share of characters are pictures; most join a meaning part and a sound part.
exercise: Fill the gap: sān ___ shū (three books) | běn | tiáo | zhāng | zhī
exercise: Fill the gap: yì ___ yú (a fish) | tiáo | běn | zhāng | bēi
exercise: Odd one out for tiáo: lù, kùzi, hé, zhǐ | zhǐ, a flat sheet of paper | lù, a long road | kùzi, a pair of trousers | hé, a long river
exercise: Nobody told you how to count snakes (shé), but you know the tiáo rule. Best guess for «one snake»? | yì tiáo shé | yì zhāng shé | yì běn shé | yì bēi shé
exercise: Which noun takes zhāng? | zhuōzi, a table | shū, a book | yú, a fish | māo, a cat
exercise: «wǒ jiā yǒu wǔ kǒu rén» means | Five people in my family | My house has five rooms | I've got five brothers | We eat five meals a day
exercise: Ming's home: mum, dad, granny, Ming and his dìdi. jǐ kǒu rén? | wǔ kǒu rén | sì kǒu rén | liù kǒu rén | sān kǒu rén
exercise: How do you order two milk teas? | liǎng bēi nǎichá | èr bēi nǎichá | liǎng tiáo nǎichá | liǎng běn nǎichá
exercise: 木 is a tree. What is 林? | A wood, two trees | A very tall tree | A tree full of fruit | A wooden table
exercise: 日 is the sun and 月 is the moon. What does 明 mean? | Bright and shining | Night, the moon's time | A whole month | The sunset
exercise: 人 and 木 make 休. What does it mean, and why? | Rest: a person leans on a tree | Woodcutter: a person chops a tree | Forest: lots of people and trees | Tall: a big person
exercise: How many trees are hiding in 森? | Three | Two | One | Four
exercise: Which character is the mountain with three peaks? | 山 | 人 | 口 | 木
exercise: Which character means «mouth» and also counts the family? | 口 | 日 | 山 | 月
exercise: 好 joins 女 and 子. What do the two parts mean? | Woman and child | Sun and moon | Tree and person | Fire and water
exercise: What do 一, 二 and 三 show? | One, two and three lines for 1, 2, 3 | Three roads that cross the city | The floors of a three-storey house | Three of the four Mandarin tones
exercise: Check the claim: «every Chinese character is a little picture» | No, most join a meaning part and a sound part | Yes, every single one of them is a picture | Yes, but only the very old ones, not the new | No, not a single character was ever a picture
test: ★★★ Josh from Bristol goes «gurt easy, ge works for everything, so yí ge shū». What would a Chinese friend usually say? | yì běn shū | yí ge shū, he's spot on | yì tiáo shū | yì zhāng shū
test: ★★★ Sienna says a river, a road and a pair of trousers have nothing in common. Why do they all take tiáo? | They're all long and thin | They're all blue | They all move along | They're all outdoors
test: ★★★ Liam from Liverpool says «boss, I'll go with yì zhāng chuáng for a bed». Is he right? | Yes: a bed has a big flat top | No: beds take tiáo, they're long | No: beds take běn, like books | No: beds take bēi, like cups
test: ★★★ A Chinese dragon is long and twisty like a snake. Which measure word does it share with a fish? | tiáo | zhī, like a cat | zhāng, like a table | běn, like a book
test: ★★★ Abby from Ohio, «lowkey confused», reads 明 as «night» because the moon is in it. What does it really mean? | Bright: the sun and moon together | Night: the moon wins over the sun | Month: it's really just the moon | Sunday: the sun comes first
test: ★★★ Noah's nan moans she's got «a thousand mouths to feed». Then Ming's mum says «wǒ jiā yǒu liù kǒu rén». Which number is real? | Six, in Ming's family | A thousand, Nan said so | Sixteen, counting the guests | Sixty, with all the cousins
test: ★★★ Freddie writes 木木 for «two trees» and asks if that's a real character. Which is the closest one? | 林, a small wood | 森, three trees | 山, a mountain | 人, a person
test: ★★★ Odd one out: 人, 山, 火, 明. Which is NOT a single picture? | 明, sun plus moon | 人, a walking person | 山, a mountain peak | 火, a burning fire
test: ★★★ Aoife from Galway says «sure, I'll guess every character from its picture, grand». What's the catch? | Most mix a meaning part with a sound part | Characters are just random scribbles | Only Cantonese characters are pictures | The pictures only work in red ink
test: ★★★ At the bubble tea shop Ming orders «sān bēi nǎichá, liǎng bēi shuǐ». How many drinks is that? | Five | Three | Two | Six
test: ★★★ Aisha's mate says yì zhī gǒu is wrong and only yì tiáo gǒu works. Which is true? | Both are used for a dog | Only zhī is right for dogs | Only tiáo is right for dogs | Neither: dogs take běn
exam: Select the measure word appropriate to the noun shū in the expression «three books». | běn | tiáo | zhāng | zhī
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the character 林, composed of two instances of 木. | A wood | A tree | A mountain | A river
exam: Determine the number of persons denoted by the utterance «wǒ jiā yǒu sān kǒu rén». | Three | Six | One | Thirty
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the composition of the majority of Chinese characters? | They combine a component of meaning with a component of sound | Each is a picture of the very thing it names | They are letters of an alphabet, like English | They are random strokes devised by one emperor
